From d99ff81f8e789b5e689175949cdc7734ccb8004f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 16:27:17 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 095/206] fix(shorebird): step the pool decoders over the
 padding, and re-route

0dfe634b44e reverted the padding because gen_kernel's own AOT snapshot
segfaulted with it. The revert message already said where the fix belonged:
all 2299 PP loads in their own snapshot are followed by nop,nop, including
the ones inside call sequences, so their readback handles padding and ours
had to as well.

SkipBackOverObjectPoolLoadPadding does that, in the three decode entry
points in instructions_arm64.cc. Every reader in that file and in
code_patcher_arm64.cc hands the decoder a fixed backward distance from a
call site -- CallPattern's pc - 2 * Instr::kInstrSize -- which lands past
the padding rather than on the load. One skip at the top of each decoder
covers all eight call sites; none of them needed touching.

DecodeLoadWordImmediate needs it twice: once at the top for a bare pool
load, and once after the orri, because the padding sits between the load
and the orri rather than after it.

The bound is Instr::kMaxObjectPoolLoadPaddingInstructions rather than an
open loop, so a run of NOPs that is not padding cannot walk off the front
of an instructions payload. It is restated in constants_arm64.h because
the AOT runtime does not build the compiler-side header that holds
kObjectPoolLoadStoreMinInstructionCount; assembler_arm64.h static_asserts
the two against each other.

With that in place the three emitters go back through PadLoadStore
(a revert of the revert). The precompiled-mode gate stays, but its comment
no longer claims to be load bearing for correctness: it is now only about
not paying two words per pool access in a JIT that never renumbers a pool.

Measured, not assumed:

  gen_kernel AOT snapshot   13.2M -> 14.1M, and it compiles a 4.2M dill
  NOPs in a hello.dart AOT  0 -> 4584   (theirs, same program: 4598)
  verify_all                8 ran, 0 failed, 0 blocked  (was 1 blocked)
  run_vm_tests              143 passed  (was 141)

op_rewrite_check.sh is no longer BLOCKED: without padding the editor
refused every site, so it had nothing to rewrite.

Negative controls, both restored afterwards:

  skip loop bound -> 0, run_vm_tests  both new tests exit 255
  skip loop bound -> 0, gen_kernel    SEGV_ACCERR, no dill

The second one rebuilds only the Product dartaotruntime against the
already-padded snapshot, so it isolates the decoder from the emitter. It
is the original regression, reproduced and then closed on one line.

Arm64PadLoadStoreRemainsDecodable and its double-word twin pin this. They
round trip against the unpadded emission rather than a table of expected
indices, so they cannot pass by agreeing with themselves, and they call
the real LoadWordFromPoolIndex with FLAG_precompiled_mode flipped rather
than restating what it emits.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

